VERDICT: BASE: abliterated. STEERING winner by selectivity margin: reft_r1 (margin=+0.110, harm-refuse=0.605, over-refuse=0.495, gibberish=0.090). DETECTION winner: diffmean (ReFT-r1 AUC=0.568 vs DiffMean AUC=0.748). DiffMean is the stronger detector, as AxBench reports. NON-SELECTIVE ARMS (margin <= 0, i.e. they refuse benign prompts at least as often as harmful ones — no steering signal at all): diffmean (-0.190), prompting (-0.150). AxBench's prompting-vs-steering claim is UNTESTABLE on this base: abliteration deleted the instruction-following refusal that ONLY the prompting arm depends on, so its loss here measures the ablation, not the method. See the aligned run for the real test. Caveats: n=200/class is one seed (SCREENING, not EVALUATION — see CLAUDE.md sec.7: n<=3 seeds cannot clear the rigor contract); the DiffMean step size (0.08) is fixed, not tuned; prompting is unconditional so its over-refusal is not gate-protected while the two activation arms' is; and the judge itself is imperfect (ROC-AUC ~0.75 on labelled data, see JUDGE_VALIDITY.md), which inflates BOTH refusal columns and is a floor under every over-refusal number here.
